.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 118 AM PDT Wed Aug 12 2026
(Today and tonight)

Today`s forecast attention turns 300 miles southwest of Point
Conception where a low pressure system is bringing up to a 15%
chance for elevated rain showers/thunderstorms Wednesday across the
Central Coast. This has the potential to be a low probability/high
impact scenario and will bring near-critical fire weather conditions
Wednesday afternoon through Thursday early morning across Monterey
and San Benito Counties. So far, lightning has remained confined to
the circulation with mid-to-high level clouds beginning to stream
in. An upstream upper-level trough, mid-level lapse rates around 7.5
degrees C/km creating sufficient MUCAPE values around 500-1000 J/kg,
and PWATs in excess of one inch are the thunderstorm ingredients.
Confidence is lowest in the lift/trigger as guidance/models are
struggling with the circulation and cutoff lows are notorious to
forecast due to their independence from the jet stream. Uncertainty
lies in what these two features will do when they collide, including
track and potential dry air entrainment which would lower rain
shower/thunderstorm chances. With moisture confined to the mid-to-
upper-levels and a dry slot in the low levels, DCAPE values of 500-
1000 J/kg will be capable of producing erratic/gusty outflow winds
of 35-45 mph with any rain showers/thunderstorms that develop. The
main concern is lightning which will pose the risk for new fire
starts and has the potential to hinder operations on the Timber
Fire. Smoke from the Timber Fire will continue to be transported
into the Central Coast via light southwesterly winds. The smell and
appearance of smoke will be the worst under the inversion at night
and in the morning. Sensible weather wise, the cooling and
moistening trend will continue through Friday with temperatures well
below normal and a deep marine layer bringing a chance of drizzle.
Minor coastal flooding is expected during the highest high tide
(evening/overnight) for bayshore locations along the San Francisco
Bay, San Pablo Bay, and Monterey Bay through Thursday morning.

